This just camelcases the names, so if `mri` returns `{ b: 'https://gitlab.com' }`, then after this transformation it becomes `{ b: 'https://gitlab.com' }`.

Then the defaults are set:
```javascript
for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    for (const name of cliOption.names) {
      options[name] = cliOption.config.default
    }
  }
  // ...
}
```

This sets both `options['b'] = 'https://github.com'` and `options['baseUrl'] = 'https://github.com'`.

Then the parsed values overwrite the defaults. If the user passed `-b https://gitlab.com`, mri returns `{ b: 'https://gitlab.com' }` which sets `options['b'] = 'https://gitlab.com'`, but `options['baseUrl']` stays as the default. The issue is that defaults get applied to all aliases before parsed values are set, so unused aliases retain the default value instead of being undefined. The fix is to set defaults only for the primary option name, then apply parsed values, so aliases that weren't used don't get a default. , name] = parsed[name]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The key insight is that parsed values need to propagate to all their aliases. So if `b` is parsed with a value, both `b` and `baseUrl` should receive that value. The fix is to iterate through each option's aliases and apply the parsed value across all of them, rather than just setting the primary key.

